#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221144 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "03fbfe2c-b12f-5220-9fae-e0dfe210a505" :question/math-academy-id "e-21857" :question/problem "Find the Fourier series of a particular solution of the differential equation\n$y'-2y=f(x)$,\nwhere the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$f(x) \\sim 3 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "We assume that a particular solution has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) + b_n \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\ny_{p}^{′} \\mid ∼\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3}) + b_{n}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3})); ∼0 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n} \\cdot (-\\frac{nπ}{3}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3})) + b_{n} \\cdot \\frac{nπ}{3}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3}); ∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞) - \\frac{a_{n}nπ}{3}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3}) + \\frac{b_{n}nπ}{3}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3})\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y'_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n(∑_(n = 1)^(∞) - \\frac{a_{n}nπ}{3}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3}) + \\frac{b_{n}nπ}{3}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3})); - 2(\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3}) + b_{n}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3})); = 3 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{12}{n^{2}π}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3})\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n-a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(\\frac{b_{n}nπ}{3} - 2a_{n})\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3}); + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(-\\frac{a_{n}nπ}{3} - 2b_{n})\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3}); = 3 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{12}{n^{2}π}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3})\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{-a_{0} = 3; \\frac{b_{n}nπ}{3} - 2a_{n} = \\frac{12}{n^{2}π}; - \\frac{a_{n}nπ}{3} - 2b_{n} = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation,\n$a_0=-3$,\nwhich means the constant term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}=-\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation,\n\n$$\nb_n=-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}\\right)\\dfrac{n\\pi}{3}-2a_n=\\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad a_n=-\\dfrac{216}{n^2\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+36)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $b_n$ with\n\n$$\nn\\ge 1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb_{n} &= -\\frac{a_{n}nπ}{6} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{nπ}{6} \\cdot (- (216)/(n^{2}π(n^{2}π^{2} + 36))) \\\\\n&= (36)/(n(n^{2}π^{2} + 36))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_{p}∼- 3 2 \\mid + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)- 216 n 2 π (n 2 π 2 + 36) \\cdot \\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3}); + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)36 n (n 2 π 2 + 36) \\cdot \\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221145 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "3944f6e4-a3a8-5bad-87c5-18227ce223bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21858" :question/problem "Find the Fourier series of a particular solution of the differential equation $y''-y=f(x)$, where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$f(x) \\sim 1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x)$.\n\n*Hint: We may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Because the forcing function has only a constant term and sine terms, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x)\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}\\sin (nπx))∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}nπ\\cos (nπx) \\\\ y_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{d}{dx}(∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}nπ\\cos (nπx))∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞) - b_{n}n^{2}π^{2}\\sin (nπx)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -b_n n^2\\pi^2 \\sin(n\\pi x) - \\left(\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x) \\right) = 1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x)\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(-n^2\\pi^2-1)\\sin(n\\pi x) = 1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x)\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{-\\frac{a_{0}}{2} = 1; b_{n}(-n^{2}π^{2} - 1) = ((-1)^{n + 1})/(n^{3}π)\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim -1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\: \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^3\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+1)} \\cdot \\sin(n\\pi x)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221147 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/id #uuid "926606c2-1e1a-5aab-8f2e-826163a204a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21875" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y'' + 9\\pi^2 y = f(x)$, where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$f(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\sin(n\\pi x)$.\nWhat harmonic of the forcing function does the natural frequency of the system match?\nAnd what is the resonant component of a particular solution?" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+9\\pi^2 y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of\n\n$$\n\\omega=\\sqrt{9\\pi^2}=3\\pi\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function\n\n$$\n\\left(L=1\\right)\n$$\n\nare\n\n$$\n\\omega_n=\\dfrac{n\\pi}{1}=n\\pi\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega_3=3\\pi\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\omega_3=\\omega\n$$\n\nthe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function. This is the answer to the first question.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is\n\n$$\nf_3(x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=x(A\\cos(3\\pi x)+B\\sin(3\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p_{3}}^{′} &= (A\\cos (3πx) + B\\sin (3πx)) + x(-3πA\\sin (3πx) + 3πB\\cos (3πx)) \\\\\ny_{p_{3}}^{″} &= -6πA\\sin (3πx) + 6πB\\cos (3πx) - 9π^{2}x(A\\cos (3πx) + B\\sin (3πx))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_{p_3}$ and $y_{p_3}$ into the equation\n\n$$\ny''+9\\pi^2 y=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left[-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)-9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}\\right]+9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{-6πA = \\frac{1}{9}; 6πB = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=-\\dfrac{x}{54\\pi}\\cos(3\\pi x)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa674a96-5fad-5391-9ecd-7207de293f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5(− 1)^{n})/(n^{2}π(nπ + 5))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220788} {:answer/id #uuid "f9acec18-0b78-5a76-b640-05ca4f225236" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (25(− 1)^{n})/(nπ^{2}(nπ + 5))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220789} {:answer/id #uuid "74f30165-bd51-51ea-a133-85c243830ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5(− 1)^{n})/(n^{2}π(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74f30165-bd51-51ea-a133-85c243830ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5(− 1)^{n})/(n^{2}π(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220790} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d9a57ed-8530-5c19-90db-08bc23e6955f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2ca3dc9-a32d-5f02-981c-e89cae88e801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (5(− 1)^{n})/(n^{2}π^{2}(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220792} {:answer/id #uuid "c84cf2bc-3717-5ce7-992c-c5cf23e100ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((− 1)^{n})/(n^{2}(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220793} {:answer/id #uuid "e597a9bb-eae7-5a94-b367-cad1ff2d843b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− ((− 1)^{n})/(n(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e597a9bb-eae7-5a94-b367-cad1ff2d843b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− ((− 1)^{n})/(n(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220794} :answer-field/id #uuid "c518bf4d-4948-5e28-9485-810284e8c4f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220791}] :question/id #uuid "7431cef2-4a56-5a41-81e1-4185e67efb64" :question/math-academy-id "q-342203" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation $y^{′} + 5y = f(x)$, where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$f(x)∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n})/(n^{2}π)\\sin (nπx)$.The Fourier series of a particular solution is\n$- (5(-1)^{n})/(n^{2}π^{2}(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221968 :knowledge-point/